/* ============ guide ============ */
function renderGuide() {
  const s = S();
  return `<div class="shell wide"><main class="main"><div class="prose">
  <h2>How ${esc(APP_NAME)} works</h2>
  <p>You buy ${s.head} feeder lambs and feed them for up to ${s.rounds} periods of ${s.daysPerRound} days, starting ${startDate().toLocaleDateString('en-US', { month: 'long', day: 'numeric' })}. Each lamb is simulated individually every day: what it eats, the energy and protein it absorbs, how much lean and fat it lays down, and whether it gets sick.</p>
  <h3>Intake</h3><p>Each lamb eats the least of three limits: the energy it needs for maintenance plus its potential gain, how much NDF its rumen can hold (about 1.8% of body weight as NDF), and a cap of 5% of body weight. On high-forage rations fill limits intake; on finishing rations energy demand does. Cold raises intake, and freshly shorn lambs eat more.</p>
  <h3>Energy</h3><p>ME is 82% of DE, and DE is TDN × 0.04409 Mcal/kg. Efficiency of ME use for maintenance (k<sub>m</sub> = 0.02·M/D + 0.5) and for gain (k<sub>g</sub> = 0.043·M/D) both rise with energy density, as in the CSIRO feeding standards. Maintenance is 0.30 MJ per kg<sup>0.75</sup> divided by k<sub>m</sub>, and rises in the cold, especially for shorn lambs. The energy in a kg of gain rises as a lamb approaches its mature size and on a high plane of nutrition, because more of the gain is fat. Ewe lambs fatten sooner than wethers, and implants make gain leaner.</p>
  <h3>Protein</h3><p>Metabolizable protein is microbial protein (13% of TDN intake, limited by rumen-degradable protein) × 0.64 plus RUP × 0.80. Lambs need about 2.6 g MP per kg<sup>0.75</sup> plus 12 g a day for wool and maintenance, and MP for the protein in their gain at 59% efficiency. If MP runs short, lean gain drops and spare energy goes to fat. Rations short of RDP also digest less well.</p>
  <h3>Gut fill and dressing</h3><p>Live weight includes gut fill, which is higher on forage. Moving lambs from hay to a high-grain ration lowers live weight for a few days even while they are gaining tissue, and lambs on high-concentrate rations dress higher. Dressing also rises with fat and with shearing.</p>
  <h3>Health</h3><ul>
    <li><b>Acidosis:</b> risk rises sharply with big jumps in concentrate, high starch and little effective fiber. Stepping into a new ration over a week and lasalocid both cut the risk.</li>
    <li><b>Urinary calculi (wethers):</b> Ca:P below 2:1, high phosphorus and no ammonium chloride (about 0.5% of DM) raise the risk.</li>
    <li><b>Enterotoxemia:</b> high-concentrate rations; two doses of CD&T protect.</li>
    <li><b>Polioencephalomalacia:</b> dietary sulfur above about 0.3% of DM, often from distillers grains.</li>
    <li><b>Copper toxicity:</b> sheep store copper in the liver. High-copper rations (a cattle mineral, for example) build it up over weeks, molybdenum slows it, and a hemolytic crisis can follow, often after handling stress.</li>
    <li><b>Coccidiosis:</b> in the first four weeks on feed, unless the ration carries lasalocid.</li>
    <li><b>Respiratory disease:</b> highest just after arrival, and in freshly shorn lambs in cold weather.</li></ul>
  <h3>Selling</h3><p>Lambs sell live on the Billings slaughter-lamb slide after ${pct(s.shrink)} shrink. Price per pound falls as lambs get heavier, so feeding past the sweet spot costs twice: more feed per pound of gain, and a lower price on every pound. Overfat lambs are docked too: yield grade 4 (about 0.36 in. of backfat) loses ${money2(s.dockYG4 * 100)}/cwt and yield grade 5 (0.46 in.) ${money2(s.dockYG5 * 100)}/cwt. Ewe lambs lay down fat at lighter weights than wethers, and high-energy rations fatten faster. Lambs that are sick (under treatment) when they ship sell at a ${pct(s.sickDock)} discount. Buyers take at least ${s.minLot} head per shipment.</p>
  ${instructorOn() ? `<h3>Notes for instructors</h3><ul><li>Equations are simplified from NRC (2007) Nutrient Requirements of Small Ruminants and the CSIRO feeding standards, and calibrated so a well-managed pen gains about 0.7–0.8 lb/day at 5–5.5 feed:gain with 2–3% death loss.</li><li>Feed composition uses typical table values and every value is editable, as are prices, costs, weather and the health risk multipliers.</li></ul>` : ''}
  </div></main></div>`;
}

